  "year": "1977",
  "subtitle": "The programmable calculator whose programs were physical cards you slid into the machine",
  "why_scout_cares": "The TI-59 (May 1977, $299.95) is the clearest mass-market expression of a strange and lovely HCI idea: your program is a physical object. The machine recorded your keystrokes into memory, and to keep a program you slid a magnetically striped card through a slot in the calculator's side — the card was the software. And then the design doubled the idea: the card you just read also slipped into the gap between the display and the keyboard, where its printed side served as the legend telling you what the five user-defined keys A–E (and their shifted A'–E' forms) did in the loaded program. Storage medium, documentation, and interface label were the same strip of plastic. Programs also shipped as plug-in 'Solid State Software' ROM modules, and from November 1977 TI ran the PPX-59 Professional Program Exchange — software distributed by mail as physical card sets, a pre-web app store. The TI-59 was Turing-complete keystroke programming that sold a calculator-size personal computer years before the PC was a category.",
  "search_terms": [
    "TI-59 1977 programmable calculator magnetic card reader",
    "Texas Instruments TI-59 Solid State Software module PPX-59",
    "TI-59 keystroke programming magnetic card documentation legend"
  ],
